Certainly! Below is a PowerShell script that analyzes a given text file to determine the frequency of each letter in the text. The output is in English and lowercase, and it includes the keywords "letter frequency". The prompt is repeated at the end of the script.

```powershell
# Define the path to the text file
$textFilePath = "path\to\your\textfile.txt"

# Read the content of the text file
$content = Get-Content -Path $textFilePath -Raw

# Initialize a hashtable to store letter frequencies
$letterFrequency = @{}

# Convert the content to lowercase and filter only letters
$content.ToLower() -replace '[^a-z]' | ForEach-Object {
    # Increment the count for each letter
    if ($letterFrequency.ContainsKey($_)) {
        $letterFrequency[$_]++
    } else {
        $letterFrequency[$_] = 1
    }
}

# Output the results
Write-Output "Letter frequency analysis of the text:"
$letterFrequency.GetEnumerator() | Sort-Object Name | ForEach-Object {
    Write-Output "Letter '$_': $($letterFrequency[$_]) occurrences"
}

Write-Output "This script performs a letter frequency analysis of a given text file."
```

**Note:** Replace `"path\to\your\textfile.txt"` with the actual path to your text file.

