{{Background and Context}}  
The landmark Supreme Court case Gideon v. Wainwright arose after Clarence Earl Gideon was charged with a felony for allegedly burglarizing a pool hall in Florida in 1961. During his trial Gideon could not afford an attorney and requested that the court appoint one for him. However his request was denied based on a Florida state law that only allowed court-appointed counsel for indigent defendants in capital cases. As a result Gideon represented himself in court and was subsequently convicted and sentenced to five years in prison. While incarcerated Gideon filed a handwritten petition to the Supreme Court of the United States arguing that his Sixth Amendment rights had been violated.

---  

{{Supreme Court Decision}}  
In 1963 the Supreme Court unanimously ruled in favor of Gideon in the landmark case Gideon v. Wainwright. The Court's decision was delivered by Justice Hugo Black who emphasized that the right to counsel is a fundamental right essential for a fair trial. The Court held that the Sixth Amendment's guarantee of counsel is a fundamental right applied to the states through the Fourteenth Amendment's Due Process Clause. This ruling overturned the Court's previous decision in Betts v. Brady which had allowed states to deny counsel to indigent defendants unless special circumstances existed. Gideon v. Wainwright thus established the principle that states are required to provide legal counsel to defendants who cannot afford an attorney in all criminal cases.

---  

{{Impact and Legacy}}  
The decision in Gideon v. Wainwright had a profound impact on the American legal system ensuring that the right to legal counsel was recognized as a cornerstone of a fair trial. This case significantly expanded the rights of defendants and reinforced the importance of the Sixth Amendment in state courts. Following the ruling states across the country were required to establish or expand public defender systems to comply with the mandate to provide legal representation to indigent defendants. The case highlighted the importance of access to justice and has been cited in subsequent rulings as a pivotal moment in the protection of defendants' rights in the United States legal system.

