  },
  "paragraph3": {
    "title": "House Edge and Player Strategy",
    "content": "The house edge in American roulette is calculated as the average loss a player can expect relative to their bet size, which stands at approximately 5.26%. This edge is maintained by paying out winning bets at odds that are slightly less favorable than the true odds of winning. For instance, a winning single number bet pays 35 to 1, while the true odds are 37 to 1. Understanding these probabilities allows players to make informed decisions about their betting strategies. Some players may choose to spread their bets across multiple numbers to increase their chances of winning, while others might focus on outside bets, such as red or black, to take advantage of the nearly 50% win probability. However, regardless of strategy, the house edge ensures that the casino remains profitable over the long term."
